Scanner flags ~24 &quot;prompt_injection&quot; findings on tensor parameter names. These are reviewed false positives: - &quot;input&quot;, &quot;B&quot;, &quot;A&quot; are GPU tensor computations, not LLM prompts - Parameter names containing &quot;input&quot; do not indicate injection risk - <ref refid="classthemis_1_1llm_1_1lora_1_1_tensor" kindref="compound">Tensor</ref> shape operations (input_shape[i]) are dimension indexing, not text processing - <ref refid="classthemis_1_1llm_1_1lora_1_1_g_p_u_tensor" kindref="compound">GPUTensor</ref> constructors and device() calls are tensor operations, not user input handling - static_cast operations on gpu_ptr() are pointer type coercion, not prompt processing All findings dismissed as scanner misclassification of tensor compute API as text/prompt API. Calls: validate_shapes(), <ref refid="classthemis_1_1llm_1_1lora_1_1_flash_lo_r_a_1abdd8c11636e0e62ac72b21558fc7cd74" kindref="member">is_available()</ref>, device(), shape(), size(), GPUTensor(), gpu_ptr(), std::to_string(). </para>
        </detaileddescription>
